# psql — real-world examples

psql is the PostgreSQL terminal interface. Connection options: `-U` username
(defaults to current OS user), `-p` port, `-h` server host, `-d` database.

## Connect to a database

```sh
psql -U postgres -h serverAddress -d dbName

# List databases on a server
psql -U postgres -h serverAddress --list
```

## Run a query and save the output

```sh
# Save query result to a file
psql -U postgres -d dbName -c 'select * from tableName;' -o fileName

# Tabular HTML output
psql -U postgres -d dbName -H -c 'select * from tableName;'

# Rows only, comma-separated -> CSV file
psql -U postgres -d dbName -t -A -P fieldsep=',' -c 'select * from tableName;' -o fileName.csv
```

## Run SQL from a file / restore

```sh
# Read commands from file
psql -f fileName

# Restore a plain-format dump
psql -f fileName.backup postgres
```

## Connection-string form

```sh
psql postgres://user:password@postgresdbIP:port/sample -c "SELECT count(id) FROM employees;"
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# On the homelab box (OpenSUSE Tumbleweed)
sudo zypper install postgresql postgresql-server
sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql

# Connect to the media metadata DB on mediaserver
psql -h mediaserver -U david -d mediastats

# Nightly CSV export for backups
psql -h mediaserver -U david -d mediastats -t -A -P fieldsep=',' \
  -c 'select * from watch_history;' -o ~/backups/watch_history.csv
```

## Handy meta-commands (inside psql)

```sql
\l          -- list databases
\c dbname   -- connect to database
\dt         -- list tables
\d table    -- describe table
\du         -- list roles
\x          -- toggle expanded output
\q          -- quit
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-c <sql>` | Run a single command and exit |
| `-f <file>` | Run commands from file |
| `-o <file>` | Send output to file |
| `-t` | Tuples only (no headers/footers) |
| `-A` | Unaligned output |
| `-P fieldsep=,` | Set field separator |
| `-H` | HTML table output |
| `--list` | List databases |
